  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the step function h(x) to minimize the maximum overlap integral C5 by combining basis expansion, entropy balancing, and gradient-based optimization. Unlike the previous approach, we use a multi-resolution strategy to systematically explore increasing resolutions beyond the parent's n=70, enabling significant improvements. The non-convex problem is addressed by:\n1. Representing h(x) using wave-like basis functions to capture periodic patterns likely in optimal solutions\n2. Balancing function entropy to prevent overly peaked solutions\n3. Projection to maintain integral constraint\n4. Stochastic optimization with adaptive constraints focusing on high-impact updates\n\nThis method avoids direct handling of the complex non-convex optimization by breaking the problem into manageable components, yielding lower bounds while respecting evaluation constraints.\n\nMathematical Specification\n1. Representation: Convert h(x) to coefficients {c_i} for 72 orthogonal basis functions (48 sinc, 16 Gaussian, 8 wavelets} ensuring L2 normalization and correct boundary conditions.\n2. Objective: Minimize the correlation coefficient \u03b2\u00b7C5 ensuring all basis updates yield physically meaningful overlaps\n3. Basis specification: \n   - Sinc: sinc(\u03c0(x-0.5*window/2)/window)\n   - Gaussian: exp(-(x-mean)**2/scale)\n   - Wavelets: mother wavelet at scale 's'\n4. Constraints enforced via orthogonal projection after gradient passes\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Use parent solution as 70-point Gaussian-smoothed base\n   - Transform to multi-resolution basis vectors (72 coefficients)\n   \n2. Optimization:\n   - Formulate gradient descent with adaptive constraint handling\n   - Use entropy balance to diversify global search\n   - Project gradients back to feasible set\n   - Update coefficients while maintaining sum constraint\n   \n3. Evaluation:\n   - Compute C5 via reconstruction then convolution\n   - Maintain numerical stability checks\n\n4. Multi-resolution extension:\n   - Double resolution when convergence plateau occurs\n   - Restart optimization with parent-like frequencies\n   - Repair integral by resampling with reduced frequency\n\nThis systematically extends the parent while ensuring integral preservation, unlike mere sampling or interpolation.\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\nStart with n=72, then expand to n=144 on improvement. Use orthogonal basis updates (72-120 points) with stochastic gradient penalty (\u03b2\u2208[0.1,0.5]). Key safeties:\n- Ensure all updates remain [0,1] via clipping and projection\n- Every N iterations repair the entire solution set\n- The initial step uses basis elements preserving integral\n\nTime budget: Use gradient-free fallback for plateaus. Total time <1300s. Budget estimation: 1000 iterations \u2248 5s at n=72, increases 4x for n=144.\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Ensure reconstruction meets 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and accurate integral\n- Self-check for overflow conditions\n- Return best solution over all resolutions tried",
